(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-2-pulmonary-anatomy-quiz"]={"title":"Pulmonary Anatomy — Anatomy Exam 2","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is the main functional difference between the pharynx and larynx?","choices":["Pharynx is a passageway for air only; larynx is a passageway for both air and food","Pharynx is a passageway for air and food; larynx is a passageway for air only and voice production","Both structures serve as identical passageways for air, food, and voice production alike","Neither structure plays any role in swallowing, airway protection, or voice production"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pharynx (throat) is a passageway for both air and food, part of both the respiratory and digestive systems, while the larynx (voice box) is a passageway for air only and is involved in voice production, part of the respiratory system only. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Does the pharynx contain vocal cords? Does the larynx?","choices":["Pharynx contains vocal cords; larynx does not","Neither structure contains any vocal cords at all","Both structures contain a full set of vocal cords","Pharynx does not contain vocal cords; larynx does"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pharynx does not contain vocal cords, while the larynx does contain vocal cords. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What structures make up the upper respiratory system versus the lower respiratory system?","choices":["Upper: trachea, bronchi, bronchioles, and alveoli; Lower: nose, nasal cavity, sinuses, pharynx, and larynx","The larynx is part of the lower respiratory system, grouped with the trachea and bronchi","Upper: nose, nasal cavity, sinuses, pharynx, larynx; Lower: trachea, bronchi, bronchioles, alveoli (inferior to the larynx)","Both divisions include identical structures, each containing the trachea, pharynx, and larynx"],"answer":2,"exp":"The upper respiratory system consists of the nose, nasal cavity, sinuses, pharynx, and larynx; the lower respiratory system consists of structures inferior to the larynx: the trachea, bronchi, bronchioles, and alveoli. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is the difference between the conducting zone and respiratory zone, and how does this differ from the upper/lower respiratory system division?","choices":["Conducting zone (larynx to alveoli) is where gas exchange occurs; respiratory zone (nose to trachea) only moves and cleans air, matching the upper/lower respiratory system division exactly","Conducting and respiratory zones describe the exact same anatomical boundary as the upper and lower respiratory system division, just using different names","Conducting zone (nose to terminal bronchioles) moves/cleans air; respiratory zone (respiratory bronchioles to alveoli) is where gas exchange occurs — this is a different division than upper/lower respiratory system","Both zones are identical to the upper/lower respiratory system division, with the conducting zone matching the upper system exactly in every structure"],"answer":2,"exp":"The conducting zone extends from the nose to the smallest (terminal) bronchioles, moving and cleaning air; the respiratory zone extends from the respiratory bronchioles to the alveoli, where gas exchange occurs; the material explicitly notes this is not the same division as the upper and lower respiratory system. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is pseudostratified, ciliated, columnar epithelium, and where is it found?","choices":["Found only in the pharynx and the thin walls of the alveoli","A type of dense irregular connective tissue, not a true epithelium at all","Found only in the alveoli and the smallest terminal bronchioles themselves","Lines most of the conducting system (except pharynx, smaller bronchi, and alveoli)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Pseudostratified, ciliated, columnar cells line most of the conducting system, except for the pharynx, smaller bronchi, and alveoli. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the function of ciliated pseudocolumnar cells (\"mucus escalator\"), versus stratified squamous cells?","choices":["Ciliated cells move mucus upward so debris can be coughed out; stratified squamous cells provide protection against abrasion","Both cell types perform an identical function, moving mucus upward so it can be coughed out","Ciliated cells provide protection against abrasion in the pharynx; stratified squamous cells move mucus upward to be coughed out","Neither cell type has any clearly described function within the respiratory tract lining"],"answer":0,"exp":"Ciliated pseudocolumnar cells move mucus in an upward manner (the \"mucus escalator\") so debris can be coughed out, while stratified squamous cells provide protection against abrasion (found in the pharynx). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What do goblet (mucous) cells produce, and where are they found?","choices":["Produce no secretions; function only in structural support of the ciliated epithelial lining and airway rigidity","Produce mucus so inhaled debris gets stuck and doesn't enter the lungs; found in the nasal cavity and lower respiratory tract","Produce hair-like cilia that beat in rhythmic waves; found only in the trachea and the larynx itself","Produce surfactant that reduces surface tension; found only in the alveoli and respiratory bronchioles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Goblet (mucous) cells produce mucus so that inhaled debris will get stuck and not enter the lungs; they are found in the nasal cavity and the lower respiratory tract. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What are vibrissae, and what stimulates the cough reflex?","choices":["Vibrissae serve no protective function at all; the cough reflex arises spontaneously without any specific trigger","Vibrissae are goblet cells lining the trachea; the cough reflex is stimulated by cranial nerve VII","Vibrissae are hairs in the nose that block inhaled debris; excess mucus stimulates the cough reflex via the vagus nerve (CN X)","Vibrissae are cilia found in the trachea; the cough reflex is stimulated by the phrenic nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Vibrissae are hairs in the nose that block some inhaled debris; excess mucus stimulates the cough reflex via the vagus nerve (CN X). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is the pathway of air through the external nares, nasal vestibule, and nasal cavity, and what role do the conchae play?","choices":["The conchae decrease surface area within the nasal cavity, have no role in warming air, and air bypasses the external nares and vestibule entirely, flowing straight into the pharynx","Air enters external nares, passes the nasal vestibule (surrounded by alar cartilage), enters the nasal cavity, and swirls around the conchae, which increase surface area, trap debris in mucus, and warm the air","Air enters the nasal cavity directly, bypassing the external nares and vestibule entirely, flowing in a straight, unimpeded path with no swirling around the conchae","Air flows in a straight line through the external nares with no swirling around the conchae, which play no role in warming, filtering, or humidifying the air"],"answer":1,"exp":"Air enters the external nares, passes by the nasal vestibule (an area surrounded by two pairs of alar cartilage), and enters the nasal cavity; as air flows in and around the nasal conchae (inferior, middle, superior), debris gets stuck in the mucus, the conchae increase surface area, and the air warms before entering the trachea. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What are the three subdivisions of the pharynx, and their boundaries?","choices":["Nasopharynx (internal nares to soft palate), oropharynx (soft palate to hyoid bone), laryngopharynx (hyoid bone to esophagus entrance)","All three subdivisions share identical boundaries, each spanning from the internal nares down to the esophagus entrance","Nasopharynx (hyoid bone to esophagus), oropharynx (internal nares to soft palate), laryngopharynx (soft palate to hyoid bone)","Only two subdivisions exist: the nasopharynx (internal nares to soft palate) and laryngopharynx (soft palate to esophagus entrance)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pharynx has three subdivisions: the nasopharynx (where the nose meets the pharynx, internal nares to soft palate), the oropharynx (where the oral cavity meets the pharynx, soft palate to base of tongue/hyoid bone), and the laryngopharynx (entrance to the trachea and esophagus, hyoid bone to entrance of esophagus). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"At what vertebral levels does the larynx begin and end?","choices":["Begins at C6; ends at T1","Begins at C1; ends at C3","Begins at C3 or C4; ends at C6","Begins at T1; ends at T4"],"answer":2,"exp":"The larynx begins at the level of vertebra C3 or C4 and ends at the level of vertebra C6. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"How many total cartilages make up the larynx, and how are they divided into unpaired versus paired?","choices":["9 total cartilages: 3 unpaired (single) and 3 paired (6 structures)","12 total cartilages: 6 unpaired and 6 paired structures total","6 total cartilages: all unpaired, with none paired at all","3 total cartilages total, all of them paired structures"],"answer":0,"exp":"The larynx contains 9 cartilages total: 3 unpaired (single) cartilages and 3 paired cartilages (6 structures total). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What are the three unpaired cartilages of the larynx, and what is distinctive about each?","choices":["Arytenoid, corniculate, and cuneiform cartilages, all three classified together as unpaired cartilages","Thyroid cartilage (contains laryngeal prominence), cricoid cartilage, and epiglottis (closes over the glottis during swallowing)","Only two unpaired cartilages exist in total: the thyroid and cricoid cartilages, with no epiglottis included","Vocal ligaments and vestibular ligaments, both classified here as unpaired laryngeal cartilages"],"answer":1,"exp":"The three unpaired cartilages of the larynx are the thyroid cartilage (contains the laryngeal prominence), the cricoid cartilage, and the epiglottis (closes over the glottis during swallowing of food). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What are the three paired cartilages of the larynx, and which is functionally most important?","choices":["Thyroid, cricoid, and epiglottis, all classified as paired cartilages","Vocal ligaments and vestibular ligaments (functionally most important)","Arytenoid (functionally most important), corniculate, and cuneiform cartilages","Only two paired cartilages exist: arytenoid and corniculate cartilages"],"answer":2,"exp":"The three paired cartilages of the larynx are the arytenoid cartilages (functionally most important, since skeletal muscles move them to abduct/adduct the vocal folds), corniculate cartilages, and cuneiform cartilages. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What do skeletal muscles moving the arytenoid cartilages accomplish?","choices":["Only produce mucus that lubricates the vocal folds during normal speaking","Abduct (open) the vocal folds during breathing, and adduct (close) them during speaking and swallowing","Control only the epiglottis's closure during swallowing, not the vocal folds","Only close the vocal folds during quiet breathing, with no opening function"],"answer":1,"exp":"Skeletal muscles move the arytenoid cartilages to abduct (open) the vocal folds during breathing, and to adduct (close) them during speaking and swallowing. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the difference between vocal ligaments (forming vocal folds) and vestibular ligaments (forming vestibular folds)?","choices":["Vocal ligaments are elastic, attached to the arytenoid cartilage posteriorly, forming the true vocal cords; vestibular ligaments are inelastic, forming the vestibular folds superior to the vocal folds","Vocal ligaments are inelastic, attached to the thyroid cartilage anteriorly, forming the false vocal cords; vestibular ligaments are elastic, forming folds inferior to the vocal folds","Neither ligament type is related to the vocal folds; both instead attach the epiglottis to the hyoid bone above the larynx, playing no role in phonation","Both ligament types form identical structures, each forming the same true vocal cords attached to the arytenoid cartilage posteriorly, with no distinct vestibular fold"],"answer":0,"exp":"Vocal ligaments are elastic and form the vocal folds (true vocal cords, attached to the arytenoid cartilage posteriorly), while vestibular ligaments are inelastic and form the vestibular folds, positioned superior to the vocal folds. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What does the pitch of vocal sound depend on, and why do adult males typically have deeper voices than females?","choices":["Pitch depends only on total lung volume, unrelated to vocal cord properties; at puberty, males simply develop greater lung capacity, producing a deeper voice","Males have thinner, shorter vocal cords than females; at puberty, only the cricoid cartilage thickens, which is what produces the deeper voice","Pitch depends on the diameter, length, and tension of the vocal cords; at puberty, males' vocal cords and thyroid cartilage become thicker/longer, producing a deeper voice","Pitch is unrelated to vocal cord anatomy entirely, depending instead solely on chest wall resonance, rib cage shape, and diaphragm strength"],"answer":2,"exp":"Pitch depends on the diameter, length, and tension of the vocal cords; at puberty, the vocal cords and thyroid cartilage of males become thicker and longer, producing a deeper voice than females (children have slender, short vocal folds, producing a high-pitched sound). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is the difference between intrinsic and extrinsic laryngeal muscles?","choices":["Neither muscle group is related to the larynx; both instead control tongue and pharyngeal muscle movement","Both muscle groups perform an identical function, positioning and stabilizing the larynx only during swallowing","Intrinsic muscles regulate vocal cord tension and open/close the glottis; extrinsic muscles position and stabilize the larynx","Intrinsic muscles position and stabilize the larynx; extrinsic muscles regulate vocal cord tension and glottis closure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Intrinsic laryngeal muscles regulate the tension of the vocal cords and open/close the glottis, while extrinsic laryngeal muscles position and stabilize the larynx. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What are the adult trachea's dimensions, and where does it bifurcate?","choices":["11 cm long, 2.5 cm diameter; bifurcates at the carina into right and left bronchi at T5","5 cm long, 1 cm in diameter; bifurcates at the level of vertebra T12","30 cm long, 5 cm in diameter; bifurcates at the level of vertebra C6","11 cm long, 2.5 cm diameter; bifurcates at the larynx into left and right bronchi"],"answer":0,"exp":"The adult trachea is 11 cm long and 2.5 cm in diameter, bifurcating at the carina into the right and left bronchi at the level of T5. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"How many tracheal cartilages does the trachea contain, and what shape are they?","choices":["Only 1 continuous spiral cartilage, not separate rings, connected by no ligaments at all","5-10 tracheal cartilages, each a complete ring, connected by loose fibrous adventitia","15-20 tracheal cartilages, each C-shaped (not complete rings), connected by annular ligaments","30-40 tracheal cartilages, each square-shaped, connected by rigid cartilaginous joints"],"answer":2,"exp":"The trachea contains 15-20 tracheal cartilages, each C-shaped (not a complete ring); annular ligaments connect one cartilage ring to another. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is the trachealis muscle, and what does it allow?","choices":["Located on the anterior side of the cartilage ring; allows only passive stretching of the trachea","Found only in the bronchioles, not the trachea; it allows bronchoconstriction and dilation there","A cartilage structure, not a muscle; it provides rigid support to the tracheal wall","Located on the posterior side of the cartilage ring; allows constriction and dilation of the trachea"],"answer":3,"exp":"The trachealis muscle is located on the posterior side of the (C-shaped) cartilage ring; it allows for constriction and dilation of the trachea. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"How does the right primary bronchus compare to the left, and what is the clinical significance?","choices":["Neither bronchus differs in any clinically relevant way, as both share an identical angle, length, and diameter","The right primary bronchus is steeper, shorter, and larger in diameter than the left, making aspiration of foreign objects into the right lung easier","Both primary bronchi are identical in angle, length, and diameter, making aspiration equally likely on either side always","The left primary bronchus is steeper, shorter, and larger in diameter than the right, making aspiration into the left lung easier"],"answer":1,"exp":"The right primary bronchus is steeper, shorter, and larger in diameter than the left primary bronchus, making it clinically easier for a person to aspirate foreign objects into the right lung than the left. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the correct branching order from primary bronchi down to bronchioles?","choices":["Secondary bronchi → primary bronchi → bronchioles → tertiary bronchi","Bronchioles → tertiary bronchi → secondary bronchi → primary bronchi","Primary bronchi → secondary bronchi → tertiary bronchi → bronchioles","Primary bronchi → bronchioles directly, bypassing secondary/tertiary"],"answer":2,"exp":"The primary bronchi branch into secondary bronchi, then tertiary bronchi, before becoming bronchioles. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the hilum, and what is the \"root of the lung\"?","choices":["Hilum is where the primary bronchus enters the lung (also the entry/exit point for pulmonary vessels); root of the lung is the combination of the bronchus, artery, and vein","Hilum and root of the lung both refer to the same single structure, the trachea, at the point where it splits into left and right bronchi","Neither term is related to lung structure; both instead describe features of the surrounding pleural membranes and mediastinal fat pad","Hilum is the apex of the lung, its most superior rounded point; root of the lung is the base, its broad inferior concave surface resting on the diaphragm"],"answer":0,"exp":"The hilum is the point where each primary bronchus enters the lung, also serving as the point of entrance and exit for the pulmonary blood vessels; the root of the lung is the combination of the bronchus, artery, and vein together. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"How many secondary bronchi does the right primary bronchus divide into, versus the left?","choices":["Right divides into two secondary bronchi (superior, inferior); left divides into three secondary bronchi (superior, middle, inferior)","Right divides into three secondary bronchi (one per lobe: superior, middle, inferior); left divides into two (superior, inferior)","Neither side divides into secondary bronchi; each primary bronchus enters its lung as one single, undivided tube","Both sides divide into an identical number of secondary bronchi, three each, regardless of each lung's lobe count"],"answer":1,"exp":"The right primary bronchus divides into three secondary bronchi, one for each of the right lung's three lobes (superior, middle, inferior lobar bronchus), while the left primary bronchus divides into two secondary bronchi, one for each of the left lung's two lobes (superior, inferior lobar bronchus). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How many tertiary bronchi (and therefore bronchopulmonary segments) does each lung have?","choices":["3 tertiary bronchi, matching the 3 lobes of the right lung only","5 tertiary bronchi and 5 bronchopulmonary segments per lung","10 tertiary bronchi and 10 bronchopulmonary segments per lung","20 tertiary bronchi per lung, with no anatomical variation"],"answer":2,"exp":"The right and left lungs each have 10 tertiary bronchi and therefore 10 bronchopulmonary segments, though there is significant anatomical variation. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Besides gas exchange, what other functions does the respiratory system serve?","choices":["Protects respiratory surfaces from dehydration, protects against pathogens, produces sound for communication, and assists in regulating blood volume/pressure/body fluid pH","Digestion of nutrients, breakdown of proteins and fats, and absorption of vitamins, functioning identically to the digestive system's role","Only gas exchange, with no other described functions beyond simply moving air into and out of the alveoli during each single breath","Only sound production for verbal communication, entirely unrelated to protection, pathogen defense, or fluid balance regulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Beyond providing an area for gas exchange, the respiratory system protects respiratory surfaces from dehydration, provides protection against invading pathogens, produces sound involved in verbal communication, and assists in regulating blood volume, blood pressure, and body fluid pH. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What bony and cartilaginous structures make up the nose?","choices":["The maxilla and mandible exclusively, with no cartilage or septal component involved at all","Only cartilage tissue, with no bony component at all, since the nose lacks nasal bones entirely","Only the frontal bone, with no other bony or cartilaginous components contributing to the nose's structure","Nasal bones, nasal septum (vomer and perpendicular plate of ethmoid), cartilage (external nares, alar cartilage), and nasal conchae"],"answer":3,"exp":"The nose consists of nasal bones, the nasal septum (formed by the vomer bone and perpendicular plate of the ethmoid), cartilage (including the external nares and alar cartilage forming the dorsum/bridge and apex/tip of the nose), and the nasal conchae. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is the key difference in apical (free) surface contact between columnar cells and pseudocolumnar cells?","choices":["Every columnar cell reaches the apical surface; not every pseudocolumnar cell reaches the apical surface","Neither cell type has any cells that ever reach the apical surface, per the material","Every pseudocolumnar cell reaches the apical surface; columnar cells largely fail to reach it","Both cell types have identical apical surface contact patterns, with every single cell reaching it"],"answer":0,"exp":"In columnar epithelium, every cell reaches the apical (free) surface, while in pseudocolumnar epithelium, not every cell reaches the apical surface (even though every cell in both types touches the basement membrane) — this is part of why pseudocolumnar epithelium falsely appears to have multiple layers. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is the vallecula, and where is it located relative to the epiglottis?","choices":["A cartilage ring found within the tracheal wall, inferior to the larynx","A ligament connecting the thyroid and cricoid cartilages at the front of the neck","A pocket posterior to the tongue, located superior and anterior to the epiglottis","A fold of the pharynx found only in the nasopharynx, superior to the soft palate"],"answer":2,"exp":"The vallecula is a pocket posterior to the tongue, located superior and anterior to the epiglottis. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 25"}]};
